nces: The variances among the groups should be equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups or levels of a factor. It is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher has categorical independent variables and a continuous dependent variable.\n- The number of groups exceeds two, as t-tests are limited to pairwise comparisons.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves:\n1. Calculating group means and overall mean.\n2. Computing the between-group and within-group variances.\n3. Performing the F-test to compare the variances.\n4. If significant, conducting post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD) to identify which specific groups differ.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA can handle multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a comprehensive view of the effects of factors and their interactions.\n- It is robust to certain violations of assumptions, particularly with large sample sizes.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of the normality and homogeneity of variance assumptions.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different unless followed by post-hoc tests.\n- It requires a balanced design for optimal performance, although unbalanced designs can still be analyzed.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\n- t-tests for pairwise comparisons.\n- Non-parametric alternatives such as Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are violated.\n- Mixed-effects models for more complex data structures.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing ANOVA, researchers should ensure proper randomization in the design, check assumptions using diagnostic plots, and consider the sample size to achieve adequate power.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMinitab is a statistical software widely used for performing ANOVA. It provides 